<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Attalid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Attalids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Attalid dynasty ruled the Hellenistic kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> from 282 to 133 BCE. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philetaerus">Philetaerus</a> and named after his father Attalus, it included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-i">Eumenes I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the Attalids made Pergamon a great cultural centre, with a famous library and the Great Altar. The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a>, bequeathed the kingdom to Rome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Attalid dynasty" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ennomus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Pergamon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
